  <text xml:lang="grc">
    <!--
  Transcriptional change categories for ECM Mark.

  Decision order (apply the first that fits; classify using only the given verse):
    1. Only the spelling differs                                  -> Orthography
    2. Same words, different order                                -> Transposition
    3. One reading has extra material and nothing else differs    -> Addition / Omission
    4. A single word changes form but keeps the same lemma        -> Inflection
    5. Anything else                                              -> Substitution
  Then decide Major vs Minor: Major if the change alters what the verse asserts.
  Explicit rules in the definitions take precedence. Otherwise, when in doubt, choose Minor.
-->
    <interpGrp type="transcriptional">
      <interp xml:id="Orthography">The same words in the same grammatical forms, written differently. This includes spelling variants, itacism and other vowel interchanges, movable ν, elision, contractions and nomina sacra. If the different spelling produces a different valid word or grammatical form (e.g. ἔρχεσθε / ἔρχεσθαι, ὑμεῖς / ἡμεῖς), it is not Orthography: classify it by the change it produces.</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Minor_Inflection">A single word changes its grammatical form while keeping the same lemma, without changing what is asserted. Suppletive forms count as the same lemma (e.g. λέγει / εἶπεν, ἔρχεται / ἦλθεν, ὁρᾷ / εἶδεν). This includes alternations among the historic present, aorist and imperfect in narrative; aorist / perfect; changes of aspect in imperatives, subjunctives, infinitives and participles; a futuristic present / future referring to the same future event; changes of mood required by the syntax (e.g. ἵνα with future indicative / aorist subjunctive); deponent middle / passive alternations; and changes of case, gender or number that keep the same referent (e.g. a different case governed by the same verb). Conative, ingressive and gnomic uses of tense are Minor. When in doubt, choose Minor.</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Major_Inflection">A single word changes its grammatical form while keeping the same lemma, and the change alters what is asserted: who acts, to whom, how many, whether something happens, or what kind of statement it is (statement, command, question or wish). This includes a change of person or number that changes the subject or referent (e.g. ἔλεγεν said by Jesus / ἔλεγον said by the disciples; αὐτῷ / αὐτοῖς), a change of case that swaps the roles of participants, a change of voice that reverses who acts, a change of mood that turns a statement into a command, and a change of tense that moves an event to a different time relative to the speaker (e.g. ἐγήγερται / ἐγερθήσεται; ἔρχεται / ἦλθεν in direct speech).</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Minor_Substitution">Words are replaced by different words, but the readings assert the same thing. This is limited to: a different function word (article, conjunction, particle, οὐ / μή, or a preposition with overlapping meaning such as εἰς / ἐν or ἀπό / ἐκ); a pronoun / a noun referring to the same participant (e.g. αὐτῷ / τῷ Ἰησοῦ); an alternative form of the same word (e.g. εὐθύς / εὐθέως); a verb prefix that does not change the sense (e.g. ἠρώτα / ἐπηρώτα, ἔρχομαι / ἐξέρχομαι); or a combination of these within the variation unit. When in doubt, choose Minor.</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Major_Substitution">Words are replaced by different words, and what is asserted changes. Any noun, verb, adjective or adverb replaced by a word with a different lemma is Major, even if the meanings are close (e.g. ἔκραξεν / εἶπεν, ὄχλος / λαός, λέγει / φησίν). This also includes a pronoun with a different referent (e.g. ὑμεῖς / ἡμεῖς), a different person, group or place name (e.g. Γερασηνῶν / Γαδαρηνῶν), a different title or description, a preposition or verb prefix that changes the relationship or direction (e.g. εἰσέρχομαι / ἐξέρχομαι, ἐκ / εἰς), a change in word order that alters what is asserted, and any combination of changes within the variation unit that includes one of these.</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Minor_Addition" corresp="#Minor_Omission">The second reading adds words that do not change what is asserted. This is limited to: articles; connective words (καί, δέ, γάρ, οὖν, ἀλλά, recitative ὅτι, ἰδού); pronouns; and names or noun phrases that fill a subject, object or addressee already implied by the verb (e.g. + ὁ Ἰησοῦς, + αὐτῷ), even if the verse alone does not identify that participant. When in doubt, choose Minor.</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Minor_Omission" corresp="#Minor_Addition">The second reading omits words that do not change what is asserted. This is limited to: articles; connective words (καί, δέ, γάρ, οὖν, ἀλλά, recitative ὅτι, ἰδού); pronouns; and names or noun phrases that fill a subject, object or addressee implied by the verb (e.g. − ὁ Ἰησοῦς, − αὐτῷ). When in doubt, choose Minor.</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Major_Addition" corresp="#Major_Omission">The second reading adds words that change what is asserted. This includes any added noun, verb, participle, adjective or adverb that does more than fill a subject, object or addressee already implied by the verb. Examples: a new or additional participant; a title or description of a participant (e.g. + υἱοῦ θεοῦ, + Χριστός, + κύριος); an adverb or phrase of time, manner, degree, place or repetition (e.g. + εὐθύς, + πάλιν, + ἐκεῖ, + ἐν ἐκείνῃ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ); a participle such as ἀποκριθείς; a negation; an action or object; or an entire clause or statement.</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Major_Omission" corresp="#Major_Addition">The second reading omits words that change what is asserted. This includes any omitted noun, verb, participle, adjective or adverb that does more than fill a subject, object or addressee implied by the verb. Examples: a participant; a title or description of a participant (e.g. − υἱοῦ θεοῦ, − Χριστός); an adverb or phrase of time, manner, degree, place or repetition (e.g. − εὐθύς, − πάλιν); a participle such as ἀποκριθείς; a negation; an action or object; or an entire clause or statement. This includes omissions caused by the scribe's eye skipping between similar words (homoioteleuton, homoioarcton) and lacunae.</interp>
      <interp xml:id="Transposition">The same words appear in a different order, and nothing else changes apart from spelling. If the reordering changes what is asserted (e.g. the roles of participants change), use Major_Substitution. If any word is added, omitted or changed as well, use the appropriate Substitution category.</interp>
    </interpGrp>
